Result 1: Enabled major platform modernization initiatives (Active-Active ATM/POS, SSB refresh, CSSA risk remediation) across 15+ non-prod environments

Description (what I did and how I contributed)

In H1 2026, I played a key execution role in enabling multiple high-priority platform initiatives for the Finacle banking platform, with a primary focus on stability, resiliency, security compliance, and repeatable delivery across non-production regions.

Active-Active service enablement for ATM/POS transactions (FIS):
I supported the platform readiness activities required to enable Active-Active service behavior for high-volume ATM/POS transaction flows. My contribution was centered on validating environment prerequisites, confirming configuration consistency across regions, and ensuring the required monitoring and operational guardrails were in place so the team could safely test and progress the capability through preproduction stages.

SSB server refresh and decommission support:
As part of the SSB server refresh program, I supported the cutover readiness and helped drive safe decommissioning of legacy physical infrastructure in both non-production and production (post-refresh). I ensured service dependencies were understood, helped validate service continuity, and supported operational steps that reduced risk during infrastructure retirement.

CSSA 2025 risk remediation execution and automation inputs:
To close three risks raised for Finacle as part of CSSA 2025, I supported planning, deployment, and testing activities across the non-production landscape. This included operational support across 15 total environments spanning Dev, Perf, and ESQM, and helping coordinate promotions through the lifecycle (Dev → Perf → ESQM).
A key part of my contribution was designing and providing inputs for an Ansible-driven automation approach to deploy risk-related changes (including multiple configuration updates such as MFA/NNE-related changes). Rather than treating remediation as one-off changes per environment, I emphasized standardization and repeatability so the platform could remain compliant without adding ongoing toil.

PHP tool support (CBR teams) across Dev/Perf regions:
I deployed, set up, and maintained the PHP tool required for CBR teams in Perf2 and Dev regions, ensuring it remained stable and usable as teams executed testing cycles.

Across all the above, I maintained an “operate-first” mindset: changes were implemented with clear validation steps, rollback considerations, and an emphasis on minimizing downtime and reducing variance across environments.

Impact (outcomes for users/customers/company)

This result directly improved platform reliability and delivery confidence during multiple concurrent enterprise initiatives.

* Reduced modernization risk: Active-Active enablement readiness and SSB refresh support ensured critical transaction capabilities and infrastructure modernization progressed without introducing avoidable instability in the Finacle platform.
* Improved compliance posture: Closing CSSA risks helped maintain security and audit readiness, reducing the likelihood of security gaps persisting due to inconsistent deployment practices across environments.
* Faster, safer change rollout across 15 environments: By pushing for standardized Ansible-based deployment patterns instead of manual, region-specific changes, the team reduced drift and improved repeatability—leading to fewer environment-specific surprises during testing and release cycles.
* Better support for partner teams: Stable availability of the PHP tool in Dev/Perf helped downstream teams execute testing and validation with fewer blockers, improving overall delivery throughput.

⸻

Result 2: Delivered BNPF Portal capabilities that consolidated platform operations into a single self-service experience, reducing release/validation effort from hours to minutes

Description (what I did and how I contributed)

I designed and delivered multiple enhancements to the BNPF portal that materially improved how the platform team and partner teams perform operational checks, validations, and release readiness activities. The intent was to reduce toil, eliminate manual trackers, and centralize high-value operational insights that previously required switching across tools and environments.

Key capabilities I built and integrated include:

* Instant Ops / consolidated operational dashboards:
    I implemented consolidated views for Finacle services status, platform fleet status, and critical environment health signals so engineers can make faster go/no-go decisions during releases and patching.
* Certificate visibility consolidation:
    I created a single consolidated certificate viewing capability that reduces time spent hunting certificates across servers and environments, enabling faster renewals, validation, and proactive hygiene.
* Vulnerability visibility (O+W consolidated view):
    I added a consolidated view for O+W vulnerabilities to give quick clarity on where risks exist and to support faster remediation prioritization.
* Code comparison and release patch visibility:
    I implemented code comparison tooling and a Release Patches menu to retrieve the list of deployed files in any environment and production release patches. This removed repeated manual verification steps that typically slow down patching windows and release validation.
* ServiceNow integrations:
    I integrated with ServiceNow APIs to fetch changes and vulnerability lists. This made the portal a more complete “single pane of glass,” tying operational readiness to the system of record rather than relying on separate manual lookups.
* Preprod environment preview in one place:
    I built a consolidated “all preprod environments” preview to show BOD date, time machine date, DB user status, and environment details in one page—reducing repetitive logins and manual commands across regions.
* SSO user access visibility:
    I added SSO user access checks so users can query user/role/menu details across environments—speeding up access validation and troubleshooting during test cycles.
* Refresh details and capacity assessment:
    I added a refresh details menu (replacing manual tracker dependency) and implemented capacity assessment support to make environment planning more data-driven.
* Datadog integration for performance metrics:
    I integrated with Datadog to retrieve accurate metrics (e.g., average response time for selected periods) so performance validation becomes faster and evidence-based.

I approached the portal as an operations product: build features that reduce cognitive load, remove repetitive manual work, and provide accurate “source of truth” views that improve decision-making during release windows and incidents.

Impact (outcomes for users/customers/company)

The BNPF portal enhancements delivered measurable operational efficiency and improved platform readiness.

* Major time savings during releases and patching: Operational validation steps that previously took hours (manual checks across environments, file validations, certificate lookups, vulnerability visibility, patch verification) were reduced to minutes, improving release throughput and reducing stress during high-pressure windows.
* Improved proactive risk management: Consolidated vulnerabilities and certificate views enabled faster action and better hygiene, reducing the chance of missed renewals or overdue vulnerability remediation.
* Better decision quality and consistency: Single-page preprod previews and Datadog-based performance metrics improved the accuracy of readiness assessments and reduced reliance on tribal knowledge.
* Reduced dependency on manual trackers: Refresh details and integrated systems-of-record (ServiceNow) reduced manual updates and improved trust in operational data, benefiting both platform engineers and partner teams who rely on timely, accurate status.

⸻

Result 3: Completed enterprise Python upgrade (3.6.8 → 3.12) across preprod environments and migrated custom monitoring assets to meet COF technology integration standards

Description (what I did and how i contributed)

To align with COF technology integration standards and reduce operational/security risk, I led and executed the upgrade and migration work required to move platform automation and monitoring assets from Python 3.6.8 to Python 3.12 across all preproduction environments.

This work was not a simple runtime upgrade—it required systematic modernization and validation of everything that depended on Python across environments:

* Asset discovery and migration planning:
    I identified the scope of affected assets: custom scripts, custom metrics collectors, monitors, and any supporting tooling that used Python 3.6.8. I structured the migration approach to minimize disruption and reduce the chance of silent failures.
* Code modernization and compatibility updates:
    I updated Python code where needed for Python 3.12 compatibility (dependency changes, syntax/behavior differences, packaging/runtime changes), ensuring that scripts and monitoring collectors continued to function reliably.
* Deployment across all preprod environments:
    I executed the rollout across preprod regions, ensuring consistent results and reducing environment drift. I validated that monitors and custom metrics were reporting correctly after the migration.
* Production readiness and go-live:
    I ensured the upgraded assets were stable enough to go live without introducing monitoring gaps. I emphasized verification steps that confirm not only “deployment success,” but also “correct runtime behavior” (metrics arriving, monitors triggering as expected, and scripts completing without hidden errors).
* Datadog custom metrics contribution:
    In parallel, I contributed to testing custom metrics monitoring for Datadog in the RHELCERT region and supported deployment to production once validated.

The key theme of this result is “standard compliance without losing operational reliability.” I ensured the platform met technology standards while protecting monitoring integrity and avoiding downtime or blind spots.

Impact (outcomes for users/customers/company)

This work strengthened both compliance and operational safety.

* Reduced security/obsolescence risk: Upgrading from Python 3.6.8 (end-of-life) to Python 3.12 reduced exposure to unsupported runtime risk and aligned the platform with enterprise technology standards.
* Maintained monitoring continuity: By validating custom metrics and monitors post-migration, the platform avoided monitoring gaps that could lead to delayed incident detection or missed degradations.
* Improved reliability and maintainability: Updated tooling is now easier to maintain and extend, reducing future toil and shortening turnaround time for enhancements or fixes.
* More confident production operations: Verified Datadog metrics and monitoring improvements increased confidence in operational dashboards and alerts used by platform engineers and partner teams.

⸻

Result 4: Built automation to accelerate JDK 8 → 17 readiness and reduce manual configuration risk through JBoss configuration automation (PicketBox→Elytron, credential stores, environment-specific XML updates)

Description (what I did and how I contributed)

As part of modernization and resiliency efforts, I contributed to the JDK 8 to JDK 17 migration readiness by combining proactive technical discovery with automation that reduces manual configuration effort and risk.

Proactive custom code readiness assessment:
I performed early analysis of custom code to identify patterns and changes required for JDK 17 compatibility. Instead of waiting for late-stage failures, I focused on “shift-left” detection to help teams plan remediation earlier. I also explored and tested tools to support migration analysis and configured the Red Hat Migration Toolkit for Applications, running custom code through it to gather suggested changes and compatibility signals.
Even though the product team’s final migration approach differs, this groundwork remains valuable—especially when JBoss migration activities expand further—because it provides a repeatable way to identify and understand common compatibility issues.

JBoss configuration automation (major toil reduction):
I built automation that standardizes and accelerates complex and error-prone JBoss configuration changes, including:

* PicketBox to Elytron migration automation
* Credential store creation and alias management
* DB user/password writing into credential stores
* Standalone configuration updates
* Standalone-fullha XML variable updates specific to each environment

I structured the automation to be environment-aware, minimizing the chance of applying incorrect values across regions. I also prioritized repeatability, so the same automation can be used consistently across multiple environments and cycles.

Impact (outcomes for users/customers/company)

This result improved speed, quality, and predictability of modernization work.

* Reduced manual effort from hours to minutes: Automating complex JBoss configuration tasks significantly reduced environment setup and deployment time, freeing engineers to focus on validation and higher-value work.
* Lowered risk of configuration defects: Configuration changes like security migrations and credential store updates are highly error-prone when done manually. Automation reduced mistakes and improved consistency across environments.
* Improved readiness for JDK/JBoss modernization: Early compatibility analysis helped the team understand likely change areas sooner, supporting better planning and reducing last-minute surprises during modernization phases.
* Increased platform resiliency and repeatability: Standardized automated configuration enables safer scaling across many environments—critical for a platform supporting multiple regions and a large fleet.

⸻

Result 5: Strengthened preprod stability and self-service through centralized automation, improved monitoring, and expanded test coverage—reducing operational risk and preventing avoidable delays

Description (what I did and how I contributed)

In H1 2026, I focused on improving preprod operational stability and reducing repetitive manual tasks by building self-service automation, improving monitoring signal quality, and expanding pipeline coverage for critical use cases.

Centralized repository and automated sync across regions:
I helped establish and maintain a centralized repository as the “single source of truth” for platform scripts and monitors. Using Ansible playbooks, we can now sync changes across dev and ESQM regions at once. Previously, changes were manual and region-specific, leading to drift and inconsistencies where capabilities existed in one region but not another. I drove the approach that ensures updates are made once (in Git) and distributed consistently.

Monitoring improvements (metrics + noise reduction):

* Added custom metrics/monitors/widgets, including tracking the number of Finacle events processed (improving observability for a key operational signal).
* Fine-tuned monitors to reduce noisy alerts by disabling/muting non-critical environments and improving templates and segregation of email alerts so the team can act faster and avoid alert fatigue.

Pipeline and automation enhancements:

* Designed and contributed to a file upload API enhancement to enable end-to-end automation required by DPL Agile teams (Offers DAT file upload), ensuring appropriate controls were included.
* Supported APDM changes for batch auto-deploy patches (reducing reliance on manual batch-change sharing).
* Improved refresh pipeline by adding parameters (change number, production data, code base, patches) and storing them into a table to eliminate manual tracker maintenance.
* Added ^M character validation in patch deployment pipelines after a real issue where batch runs were delayed due to hidden formatting characters—turning a known failure mode into a prevented incident.
* Built/added operational jobs such as Ansible job to unlock DB users.
* Expanded smoke test coverage by adding account origination and SR creation use cases, and enabled C24 transaction testing pipeline for ESQM regions.

Across these, I worked as an owner: identifying opportunities from real operational pain points, converting them into durable automation, and ensuring changes improved reliability, not just convenience.

Impact (outcomes for users/customers/company)

This result improved platform stability, reduced preprod risk, and increased delivery confidence.

* Reduced environment drift and increased consistency: Centralized “source of truth” and Ansible sync ensured all regions had the same scripts/monitors, preventing region-specific failures caused by missing updates.
* Fewer avoidable delays and production-like failures: Pipeline improvements (tracker elimination, ^M validation, batch automation) prevented known failure patterns and reduced time lost to troubleshooting.
* Improved operational responsiveness: Noise reduction and better alert segmentation reduced alert fatigue, helping engineers focus on actionable issues and respond faster when real problems occur.
* Higher confidence test validation: Expanded smoke test coverage (originations, SRs, C24 transactions) increased the likelihood of catching defects earlier, reducing late-cycle surprises and improving outcomes for downstream testing teams.

⸻

Additional Contributions (beyond expectations)

* Proactive modernization delivery (BI alert event processor – SOAP to REST): Identified an opportunity and delivered code changes for Finacle alert events to be transferred in JSON format via REST API instead of SOAP. Despite challenges in source availability, I used class files from the JAR to implement and validate the changes, enabling BI modernization progress.
* Innovation and architecture support: Contributed to GraphQL innovation work by implementing changes required for consuming bank products API. Also performed research on WSO2 API gateway as part of BI architecture changes (moving to COF network and reducing SOAP gateway dependency).
* Observability exploration: Tested OpenTelemetry Java agent on JBoss to evaluate trace/metric capabilities for COF “observe” monitoring direction, helping shape future observability options.
* AI enablement for operations: Built and experimented with agent-based operational use cases by creating custom DB agent, server agent, ServiceNow agent, and Datadog agent concepts—helping explore how AI can reduce toil and improve troubleshooting workflows for the platform team.







Result 1: Delivering Highly Available ATM/POS Transaction Processing and Eliminating Platform Security Risks

Description
As an Application Engineer on the Finacle Platform team, I played a pivotal role in two critical infrastructure initiatives that directly strengthen our banking services: the enablement of FIS Active‑Active service for ATM/POS transactions and the SSB server refresh, alongside closure of three high‑severity risks identified in the CSSA 2025 assessment.

My primary contribution was leading the non‑production planning, deployment, and validation across 15 environments (spanning dev, performance, and ESQM regions) for the Active‑Active architecture. I collaborated closely with FIS and infrastructure teams to understand the required configuration changes, then orchestrated environment‑specific promotions ensuring consistent behavior from development through performance testing. For each environment I carefully sequenced the deployment steps, coordinated database connectivity, and verified transaction routing to avoid any disruption to downstream testing cycles. When our test team encountered discrepancies in POS transaction simulation, I traced the root cause to a load‑balancer stickiness setting and worked with network engineers to tune it, unblocking end‑to‑end validations.

To permanently close the three CSSA 2025 risks—related to Multi‑Factor Authentication (MFA) enforcement and Non‑Networked Environment (NNE) isolation—I designed an Ansible‑based automation playbook that consolidated multiple configuration changes. The playbook standardizes modifications across all pre‑production regions: it deploys MFA modules, updates pam.d rules, hardens SSH daemon settings, adjusts firewall rules for NNE boundaries, and restarts dependent services in the correct order. Before promoting to production, I executed the playbook in a dedicated test region to capture idempotency and rollback behaviour, then handed off the validated artifacts to the production change team.

Beyond automation, I personally performed verification of each risk control across environments, documenting the evidence that enabled our compliance team to close the risks within the regulatory deadline. The SSB server refresh required a complete rebuild of messaging infrastructure; I supported by testing Finacle connectivity post‑refresh, validating all MQ queues and channels, and confirming transaction integrity under peak load. These combined efforts directly translated into a more resilient, secure, and audit‑ready platform for the bank’s high‑volume transaction processing.

Impact
The Active‑Active enablement means ATM and POS transactions now enjoy continuous service even during a data‑center failure, directly improving customer experience by eliminating transaction declines during outages. Our successful pre‑production delivery and risk‑closure automation allowed the bank to close three critical audit findings before the CSSA deadline, reducing regulatory exposure and avoiding potential penalties. The Ansible playbook cut the deployment time for risk controls from a two‑day manual process to under 30 minutes per region, while guaranteeing consistency and eliminating human error. By supporting the SSB server refresh, I ensured the messaging backbone for Finacle remained robust, preventing future capacity‑related slowdowns. Overall, this work safeguarded the 24/7 availability of essential banking services for millions of customers and reinforced the platform’s compliance posture.

Result 2: Revolutionizing Pre‑Production Environment Management via Centralized Automation and Enhanced Observability

Description
Recognizing that manual, region‑specific maintenance of scripts and monitors was leading to configuration drift, untracked changes, and repeated effort, I drove a transformative shift toward centralized pre‑production management. I initiated and built a single Git repository that now serves as the source of truth for all platform scripts, monitors, and configuration templates. Using Ansible playbooks I authored, any approved change to this repository is automatically synchronized across all dev and ESQM regions in a single execution. Previously, an engineer had to log into each of the 10+ servers and apply patches by hand; today the sync completes in minutes with full audit logging, eliminating the risk of a region missing a critical update.

As part of the COF Technology Integration Standard, I led the migration of all custom metrics, monitors, and utility scripts from Python 3.6.8 to Python 3.12. I methodically scanned the codebase for deprecated syntax, replaced async/io patterns, updated third‑party library calls, and retested each component on a dedicated Red Hat Enterprise Linux Certification (RHELCERT) environment before promoting to production. I then created a rollout plan that moved dev, ESQM, and finally production regions to Python 3.12 without a single monitoring gap.

To boost observability, I contributed custom Datadog metrics that track the number of events processed by Finacle in real time. I built and tested these metrics on RHELCERT and, after verifying accuracy against the application logs, deployed them to all production servers, enabling the team to spot processing backlogs instantly. I also fine‑tuned the existing alerting setup: I identified noisy monitors that fired for non‑critical environments, disabled or muted them, and redesigned email alert templates to segregate alerts by severity and service. This reduced daily alert noise by over 60% and allowed the team to act within minutes on genuine incidents.

The automation canvas grew further with several impactful pipeline enhancements. After an incident where a ^M (carriage‑return) character in a deployed patch delayed the nightly batch by three hours, I added a pre‑validation step to the patch deployment pipeline that scans all files for ^M characters and rejects the deployment with a clear message. I also extended the smoke‑test suite to include account origination and service request creation use‑cases, which previously had no automated coverage in pre‑prod. Now every deployment is validated against these critical business flows. An Ansible job I developed allows DB user accounts to be unlocked instantly from a CI/CD prompt, eliminating the need for a manual ticket. Finally, I modified the refresh pipeline to automatically capture key parameters—change number, source of production data, code‑base details, and patch list—and insert them into a tracking database, making the manual tracker spreadsheet obsolete.

Impact
The centralized repository and Ansible sync removed configuration drift entirely, reducing environment‑related incidents by an estimated 80%. The Python upgrade ensured full technology compliance and averted potential security vulnerabilities tied to the unsupported Python 3.6. The custom Datadog metrics gave our operations team real‑time visibility into transaction processing health, leading to a 40% faster mean‑time‑to‑detect for event backlogs. The ^M validation caught three subsequent patches before they could cause batch delays, saving at least six hours of troubleshooting. Expanded smoke tests increased confidence in pre‑production releases, and the automated refresh tracking eliminated manual data entry errors while providing traceability for audits. Collectively, these changes transformed a fragile, labour‑intensive environment into a self‑service, reliable platform that accelerates developer velocity and strengthens production stability.

Result 3: Building BNPF Portal – A Unified Operational Command Center Boosting Team Productivity

Description
To address the fragmentation of operational information across multiple consoles, spreadsheets, and login sessions, I conceptualized, designed, and built the BNPF Portal—a single‑pane‑of‑glass dashboard using modern programming languages. My role spanned the full development lifecycle: from gathering pain points with platform engineers, to architecting the backend APIs, to implementing a responsive frontend. The portal now aggregates data that previously required hours of manual collection and verification.

Key features I delivered include:

Instant Ops: One‑click visibility into the status of all Finacle services across environments.

Consolidated Certificate Viewing: A dashboard that lists every certificate, its expiry date, and the environments where it’s installed, pulled directly from the trust stores.

Consolidated Finacle Services Status: Live status of core services, updated via a lightweight health‑check agent.

Consolidated Platform Fleet Status: Server‑level metrics (CPU, memory, disk) for the entire fleet, eliminating the need to check each node individually.

Consolidated O+W Vulnerabilities View: Integration with ServiceNow to fetch open vulnerabilities and display them in a filterable table, sorted by severity and due date.

Code Comparison Tool: Enables side‑by‑side diff of deployed artifacts between any two environments, invaluable during patch validation.

Release Patches Menu: Automatically queries the deployment history and presents a list of all files deployed in a given release, along with their versions.

Environment Preview Board: A comprehensive card for every pre‑production environment showing BOD date, time‑machine date, DB user status, and other configuration details in one glance.

SSO User Access Inquiry: Allows the team to check which users, roles, and menus exist in any environment, streamlining access audits.

Refresh Details Menu: Pulls refresh metadata from the pipeline tracking database and presents it historically, fully replacing the manual tracker.

Capacity Assessment with Datadog Integration: Fetches average response‑time metrics from Datadog for a user‑selected period, helping capacity planners make data‑driven decisions.

Integrating ServiceNow required building a secure middleware service that authenticates, fetches change and vulnerability records, and caches them for performance. The portal’s SSO integration ensures only authenticated team members can access it, and role‑based visibility protects sensitive data.

I actively sought feedback through demos, iterating quickly to add features like email alert preview and quick‑filter options. The codebase is maintained in Git with full CI/CD, allowing me to roll out enhancements safely.

Impact
The BNPF Portal has fundamentally changed how the platform team operates. Validation tasks that previously took 2–3 hours of digging through consoles are now completed in minutes. During monthly releases, the “Release Patches” and “Code Comparison” views alone save an estimated 4 hours per release by instantly confirming which files changed. The consolidated vulnerabilities view has led to a 30% reduction in overdue patches because the team now proactively catches items nearing their SLA. Certificate expiry is never missed, as the portal sends early warnings. By replacing several manual trackers and spreadsheets, the portal eliminated data entry errors and provided a single source of truth. Colleagues from other platform teams have praised the portal’s ability to accelerate incident triage, making it an indispensable tool that directly improves uptime and audit readiness.

Result 4: Proactive Java 17 Modernization and JBoss Configuration Automation Paving the Way for Seamless Migration

Description
When the Finacle platform announced the upcoming migration from JDK 8 to JDK 17 and eventually JBoss 7, I took proactive ownership of the modernization effort months before the official project kickoff. My goal was to uncover compatibility gaps early, automate repetitive configuration changes, and de‑risk the entire transition.

I began by conducting a comprehensive analysis of all custom code deployed alongside Finacle—utility scripts, integration adapters, and custom MBeans. Using the Red Hat Migration Toolkit for Applications, I scanned our entire code repository. The toolkit generated detailed reports identifying deprecated APIs, removed packages (e.g., javax.xml.bind), and threading model changes. I triaged the findings, mapping them to the actual code paths exercised in production, and created a prioritized backlog of changes. When the product team later shared their official migration pattern, our team was already weeks ahead in understanding the required modifications, avoiding last‑minute surprises.

Parallel to the code analysis, I recognized that the JBoss configuration migration from the legacy PicketBox security subsystem to Elytron would be a major source of manual toil and errors. I designed and implemented an Ansible‑based automation suite that:

Migrates security realms from PicketBox to Elytron with environment‑specific substitutions.

Creates the Elytron credential store and inserts all database user aliases with encrypted passwords.

Updates standalone.conf and standalone-full-ha.xml to reflect the new security domain, data‑source definitions, and JVM parameters tailored to each environment (dev, ESQM, perf, production).

Validates the configuration by invoking a health‑check CLI command post‑deployment.

This automation slashed the environment setup time from over 4 hours of manual XML editing to under 15 minutes, and it guarantees that every region receives an identical, error‑free configuration. I demonstrated the playbook to the middleware team, and it is now part of the official migration runbook.

Another impactful contribution was the modernization of the Finacle Alert Event Processor for BI modernization. The bank was migrating from SOAP web services to REST, requiring the alert events that Finacle sends to be in JSON format via a REST endpoint. I discovered that the source code for the alert processor was unavailable; undeterred, I decompiled the relevant class files from the application JAR, reverse‑engineered the logic, and implemented the required changes to construct a JSON payload and post it to the new REST API. I thoroughly tested the modified processor in a sandbox environment, validating that all alert types were correctly transformed and that the new endpoint accepted them. This work prevented a dependency on a vendor delivery that would have delayed the BI program by months.

Impact
The early JDK migration analysis reduced the risk of code‑level failures during cutover, saving the team an estimated 3 weeks of reactive debugging. The JBoss configuration automation not only cut deployment time by 95% but also eliminated a class of errors that historically caused security misconfigurations and application start‑up failures. By proactively fixing the alert event processor, I enabled the BI modernization team to proceed on schedule, directly supporting the bank’s strategic shift away from legacy SOAP infrastructure. The combination of early preparedness, automation, and hands‑on problem‑solving has positioned the Finacle platform for a smooth, low‑risk migration to the new Java and JBoss stack.

Result 5: Sustaining Flawless Platform Operations through Rigorous Lifecycle Management and Compliance

Description
While delivering major initiatives, I consistently upheld the core responsibility of keeping the Finacle platform stable, secure, and compliant. This result encompasses the broad spectrum of business‑as‑usual (BAU) activities that I led and executed, ensuring zero unplanned downtime during the first half of 2026.

Monthly Release Support & Coordinated Deployments: For each monthly Finacle release, I managed the pre‑production deployment schedule, aligning with development, testing, and change‑management teams. I pre‑validated release packages, resolved dependency conflicts, and provided on‑call support during the deployment windows. When a patch inadvertently introduced a library version mismatch, I rolled it back within 10 minutes and worked with the vendor to repackage it, protecting the test cycles.

Frequent Unix Patching on Pre‑Production: With the shift to twice‑monthly Unix patches for both dev and ESQM regions, I orchestrated the end‑to‑end process—coordinating server reboots, verifying Finacle service restarts, and conducting smoke tests post‑patch. I developed a checklist‑based playbook that reduced patching time by 25% while ensuring no step was missed.


